  "result": "Implemented `catalog/service.py` and all four tests pass:\n\n```\n....                                                                     [100%]\n4 passed in 0.02s\n```\n\n**What I built.** A `Catalog` backed by an insertion-ordered dict, so `list()` returns creation order for free. `get`, `update`, and `list` return copies so callers cannot mutate internal state. `update` validates field names before looking up the record, so an unknown field raises `ValueError` even when the id is also missing. `NotFound` carries the offending id as its message.\n\n**Approach chosen.** I picked Approach A, sequential integers, because the spec defines a single in-process, in-memory catalog with no cross-process or client-side id generation, so UUIDs' coordination-free uniqueness buys nothing here while short sortable ids are easier to read and debug. The per-catalog counter only ever increments, so deleted ids are never reused and every id stays unique and stable for the record's lifetime.",
